/**
@mainpage vkmEngine API Reference

A C++17 engine with an OpenGL 4.3 backend, for Windows and Linux, that **runs
projects**. It holds no game of its own: a game is a directory - a
`project.json`, its scenes and assets, and gameplay code built into its own
module - and the same four executables run any of them.

This is the generated reference for the types a project compiles against. It is
not the manual. The manual is in `docs/`, and it is the better place to start:

- @b guides/engine.md - what the engine is, what it refuses, what is settled
- @b guides/getting-started.md - building a game against a released SDK
- @b reference/ - how each subsystem actually works, in prose

@section api_where Where to look first

| If you are | Start at |
|------------|----------|
| Writing gameplay | Vkm::Engine::Behavior, Vkm::Engine::BehaviorContext |
| Building a world in code | Vkm::Engine::Scene, Vkm::Engine::EntityId |
| Loading or making assets | Vkm::Engine::ResourceManager, Vkm::Engine::Handle |
| Reacting to things | Vkm::Engine::EventBus, Vkm::Engine::FrameContext |
| Writing a system | Vkm::Engine::System, Vkm::Engine::SystemStage |

@section api_conventions Conventions worth knowing before you read anything

- **Forward is -Z, screen-right is +X, up is +Y** - right-handed, glm's own
  convention. Ask through Vkm::Engine::Math::computeForward and friends rather
  than writing an axis out by hand.
- **Components are plain structs.** There is no registration step and no base
  class; `scene.add<T>(entity, ...)` is the whole mechanism.
- **Assets are referenced by Vkm::Engine::Handle, never by pointer**, and
  serialized by *name* - the name is the identity that survives a save.
- **Systems never call each other.** Vkm::Engine::FrameContext is the only
  per-frame channel between them: references are services, pointers are
  per-frame products that are null until their producer has run.

@section api_stability What is stable

Everything under `Vkm::Engine` that a project can include is public surface.
`Vkm::GL` is the platform layer and is versioned separately. Anything in a
`detail` namespace is not public and will move without notice.
*/
